CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ more-constrained overload must win: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.constr.order]/1 + [over.match.best]/2.6: of two otherwise
indistinguishable function-template overloads, the more-constrained is chosen.
Cheap subsumes Cheap||Rare, so f<Cheap> wins for an int argument (returns 1,
matching g++); assertion 2 (a wrong value) must FAIL, proving non-vacuity.

CORE (was KNOWNBUG): the pre-instantiation concept-subsumption filter in
cpp_typecheck_resolvet::resolve now orders candidates by the normal forms of
their associated constraints ([temp.constr.order]/1, DNF/CNF over atomic
constraints) rather than by a textual comparison of constraint names, so the
more-constrained f<Cheap> is selected.  Filtering before instantiation also
avoids the colliding instance symbols of the two same-signature overloads.
